---
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';
import { href } from '../../i18n/routes';

const lang = 'en' as const;
const page = 'configure' as const;
---
<Docs lang={lang} page={page}
  title="Configure a repository"
  description="Configure a repository with one file on the default branch. Understand the three configuration levels, the settings that matter most, and the important caveats.">
<div class="dhead stack g12">
          <p class="crumb"><b>docs</b> / configure</p>
          <h1>Configure a repository</h1>
          <p class="lede measure">Repository configuration lives in a single file, <code>.conventional-comments.json</code>, on the default branch. You only need to specify what you want to change. Anything you leave out keeps the product default.</p>
        </div>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2>The smallest useful configuration</h2>
          <pre class="code"><span class="tk-k">&#123;</span>
  <span class="tk-k">"$schema"</span>: <span class="tk-v">"https://conventionalcomments.io/schema/v1.json"</span>,
  <span class="tk-k">"version"</span>: <span class="tk-v">1</span>,
  <span class="tk-k">"mode"</span>: <span class="tk-v">"assist"</span>
<span class="tk-k">&#125;</span></pre>
          <p>The <code>$schema</code> entry is more than metadata. Your editor uses it to autocomplete available settings, show documentation on hover, and flag invalid values such as <code>"enforc"</code> before you commit them.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2>Three configuration levels</h2>
          <p>The repository file is only one of three configuration sources. All three are resolved together, in a fixed order.</p>
          <div class="scroller">
            <table class="t">
              <thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Who controls it</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td class="m">floor</td><td class="d">The organization, through deployment — never through a repository file.</td><td class="d">Sets the minimum mode and defines rules that repositories cannot disable. A repository may be stricter, but never more permissive.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m">organization</td><td class="d">A shared configuration document fetched from the URL defined by the floor.</td><td class="d">Provides the common baseline. Changing <code>mode</code> here takes effect across the organization, including on pull requests that are already open.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m">repository</td><td class="d"><code>.conventional-comments.json</code> on the default branch.</td><td class="d">Applies local adjustments, such as exempting bots, enabling an optional label, or silencing a diagnostic.</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <div class="callout acc">
            <span class="ct">the floor is delivered through two channels, and they must agree</span>
            <p style="margin:0">The extension reads the floor from your browser's enterprise policy. The server reads the same floor from its own deployment configuration. That duplication is intentional. A server has no browser, so it cannot read browser policy. If the floor existed only there, the server could not enforce it — and the browser extension is deliberately a component that users can bypass. The server therefore publishes a fingerprint of the floor it is applying. If the two channels disagree, the mismatch becomes visible instead of going unnoticed.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2>The settings worth knowing</h2>
          <p>There are twenty-five settings in total. These are the ones that affect behaviour rather than presentation.</p>
          <div class="scroller">
            <table class="t wide">
              <thead><tr><th>Key</th><th>Default</th><th>What it controls</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td class="m">mode</td><td class="m d">assist</td><td class="d"><code>off</code> · <code>assist</code> · <code>warn</code> · <code>enforce</code>. Read the adoption guide before moving beyond <code>warn</code>.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m">formatSeverity</td><td class="m d">warn</td><td class="d">Whether malformed comments can cause the check to fail. Most teams leave this unchanged: unresolved blocking points, rather than formatting mistakes, should normally be what prevents a merge.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m">resolverOverrideGroup</td><td class="m d">empty</td><td class="d">Defines who may override a resolution and record a <code>decision</code>. <strong>Required before using <code>enforce</code>.</strong> When left empty, both escape hatches are unavailable.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m">exemptUsers</td><td class="m d">empty</td><td class="d">Users whose comments are never validated. Values are matched against platform logins case-insensitively.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m">toolCommands</td><td class="m d">empty</td><td class="d">Defines comments intended for bots or tools. See the warning below.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m">labels</td><td class="m d">13 labels</td><td class="d">Per-label overrides. Only include the labels you want to change.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m">severities</td><td class="m d">product defaults</td><td class="d">Changes the severity of individual diagnostics or silences them entirely.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="m">coreMinVersion</td><td class="m d">1.0.0</td><td class="d">Requires a minimum core version across the organization. This is different from the fingerprint, which only reports a mismatch.</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <div class="callout blk">
            <span class="ct">one setting deserves special attention</span>
            <h4><code>toolCommands</code> is empty by default. That wasn't always the case.</h4>
            <p>Slash commands such as <code>/rebase</code> were previously recognized through a built-in list. That list no longer exists. In strict mode, a bare <code>/rebase</code> is therefore treated like any other non-conforming comment unless the repository explicitly declares it. Adding <code>"/*"</code> exempts every slash command. Bot accounts are declared individually and must match exactly, for example <code>"@dependabot"</code>.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="dsec">
          <h2>Two behaviours worth knowing</h2>
          <ul class="bul">
            <li><strong>Unknown settings are ignored with a warning rather than rejected.</strong> A recognized setting with an invalid value is different: it invalidates the entire document. If the configuration cannot be interpreted reliably, none of it is applied.</li>
            <li><strong>A <code>configUrl</code> declared in the repository file is ignored.</strong> The organization configuration URL comes exclusively from the floor. Allowing a repository to redirect it would effectively allow that repository to choose its own organization-wide rules.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>
        <div class="pager"></div>
</Docs>
